.TH RM 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
rm \- remove files or directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rm
[\fB\-r\fR|\fB\-R\fR] [\fB\-f\fR] [\fB\-i\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fIFILE\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Remove (unlink) each FILE. By default, does not remove directories; use \fB\-r\fR to remove a directory and its contents.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-r, \-R, \-\-recursive
Remove directories and their contents recursively.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Ignore nonexistent files, never prompt.
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-interactive
Prompt before every removal.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR (print a usage line and exit) and \fB\-\-version\fR. Commands that take file/path arguments also accept \fB\-\-\fR to mark the end of options, so a file literally named e.g. \fB\-oddfile\fR can still be referenced.
.SH NOTES
On Linux, recursive removal is TOCTOU-hardened (common::safewalk) against a directory being swapped for a symlink mid-traversal; other targets use a plain path-based walk. See mitos-utils-security(7).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on a general failure (a listed file could not be processed, etc.), 2 on a usage error (missing or invalid arguments).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7),
